  "title": "Effect of Change in Concentration on Equilibrium",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A chemical system at equilibrium can be temporarily shifted out of equilibrium by adding or removing one or more of the reactants or products. The concentrations of both reactants and products then undergo additional changes to return the system to equilibrium."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3zn14scri6djxdq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Consider the reversible reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4rhdhamt1qpi34x",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ze2khna44506vqn",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8bgckm52bk9hz43",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm A\\;+\\;\\mathrm B\\;\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm C\\;+\\;\\mathrm D",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Suppose the applied stress is the addition of a reactant, A or B. Le Châtelier's principle states that the system will respond in a way that counters this stress. In this example, the stress increased the concentration of a reactant, so the system will shift in the forward direction to decrease the concentration of reactants. Similarly, if either product (C or D) is added, the system will shift in the reverse direction to counter the stress by decreasing the concentrations of the products. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "oixofktpwhu8nik",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Chemical species can also be removed from the system. If the concentration of reactant (A or B) is decreased, the system will shift in the reverse direction to counter the stress by increasing the reactant concentrations. If product is removed, the system will shift in the forward direction to counter the stress by increasing the concentration of products."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3bzkh7jqhe7mc06",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
